WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:02.567
-I thought you didn't go
to Nevermore.

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:03.567
-I didn't.

00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:05.934
You dad got all the brains.

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:07.834
But I used to drop in on him.

00:00:07.834 --> 00:00:09.300
Usually from the ceiling

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.400
with a dagger clutched
between my teeth,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.734
just to keep him on his toes.

00:00:12.734 --> 00:00:14.234
-Of course.

00:00:14.234 --> 00:00:16.434
-He filled me in on
what's been going on.

00:00:16.434 --> 00:00:20.133
Monsters, murder, mayhem.

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:21.234
What fun!

00:00:21.234 --> 00:00:22.367
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:24.434
We're here with Fred Armisen,
everybody.

00:00:24.434 --> 00:00:26.400
That was a clip from
"Wednesday,"

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.634
currently streaming on Netflix.

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:31.534
What a cool project,
directed by Tim Burton.

00:00:31.534 --> 00:00:32.634
-Can't believe it.

00:00:32.634 --> 00:00:34.367
Can't believe I got to do it.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:37.234
As soon as they asked me
to do it, I --

00:00:37.234 --> 00:00:38.400
The first thing I remember
offering was like,

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.033
"I will shave my head
to do this."

00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:41.100
And I did.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.567
I really wanted to, like,
get into it.

00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:44.167
-'Cause sometimes when you say,
"I'll shave my head,"

00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:45.400
they'll say,
"Oh, don't be ridiculous.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.567
We'll just put on a bald cap."

00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:48.267
-Yeah, but you know those things
take forever.

00:00:48.267 --> 00:00:50.167
-It is.
As an actor, it's almost --

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:51.267
if you're willing to
shave your head,

00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:53.067
you've saved so much time.
-Absolutely.

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:56.167
And it always looks kinda weird.
You can always kind of tell.

00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:58.534
No disrespect to makeup artists
around the world.

00:00:58.534 --> 00:00:59.901
You're all so talented.

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:01.634
[ Laughter ]

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:02.834
Phew.

00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:05.267
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:07.667
-So you shaved your head
and Uncle Fester --

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.467
this is, you know,
an iconic role.

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:10.934
How do you approach
Uncle Fester?

00:01:10.934 --> 00:01:13.868
-You know, there was kind of
like an "SNL" thing to it

00:01:13.868 --> 00:01:16.968
where I did remember the TV show
and I just

00:01:16.968 --> 00:01:19.534
tried to do it like I was doing
the original TV show.

00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:23.400
It's almost like representing
the original character.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.667
-That's really fun.
-Yeah.

00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:26.067
And Tim Burton was great.

00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:28.100
He's like, you know,

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.200
I don't know how
you picture him,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.501
but I thought he'd be like,
a little bit sort of quiet.

00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:36.334
But he's kind of like, a pretty
tough, confident guy.

00:01:36.334 --> 00:01:39.100
He's not a New Yorker,
but he seems like a New Yorker.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.434
-That's so funny, yeah.

00:01:40.434 --> 00:01:42.300
I think of him way differently,

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.968
that he would be sort of like
quirky and like,

00:01:43.968 --> 00:01:46.167
would give your notes through
a little cupboard door.

00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:47.767
-That's --

00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:49.133
That's exactly what I mean,
yeah.

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:52.334
-One of the other things I want
to talk to you about,

00:01:52.334 --> 00:01:54.434
because "Documentary Now,"
we just had another season,

00:01:54.434 --> 00:01:55.601
our fourth season

00:01:55.601 --> 00:01:58.567
of a very unique
and special show.

00:01:58.567 --> 00:01:59.901
[ Applause ]

00:01:59.901 --> 00:02:01.601
It was sort of inspired by
this sketch

00:02:01.601 --> 00:02:03.767
you and I wrote together
where you played Ian Rubbish,

00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:06.100
a punk rocker from the '70s.
-Yeah.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.000
-The only punk rocker
who liked Margaret Thatcher.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.400
-Yeah.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.100
-And it's a sketch
I'm really proud of.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.968
One of the things about
working with you was always,

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:14.267
especially when it
was music stuff,

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.567
is you would be
incredibly specific

00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:17.667
about the music.

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:19.067
If it was punk songs
from the '70s,

00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:20.634
you tried to write
great punk songs,

00:02:20.634 --> 00:02:23.067
and they are still catchy and
I still love listening to them.

00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:25.934
The other thing is, you can
do a really good British accent,

00:02:25.934 --> 00:02:27.901
and I feel like watching
a bad one...

00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:29.567
-That's nice of you to say.

00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:30.734
I do like doing it.

00:02:30.734 --> 00:02:32.167
I want to add one thing.

00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:35.400
Seth wrote the lyrics
to those Ian Rubbish songs.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.801
[ Applause ]

00:02:36.801 --> 00:02:38.100
-We're a very good match.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.467
-Honorary punk.
-Honorary punk.

00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:44.501
You've also made an observation
to me before about how --

00:02:44.501 --> 00:02:46.634
because I think it happens more
this way than the other --

00:02:46.634 --> 00:02:48.534
British actors playing
Americans.

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:51.067
-Yeah, sometimes
when you watch something,

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:53.567
you can tell that it's
a British person,

00:02:53.567 --> 00:02:55.267
'cause it's like,
a non-specific accent,

00:02:55.267 --> 00:02:57.067
them doing an American accent,

00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:59.634
and like, they try to toughen it
up a little bit.

00:02:59.634 --> 00:03:02.667
[In American accent] "Hey, I'm
gonna read the newspaper.

00:03:02.667 --> 00:03:04.534
I'm gonna try to
smoke a cigarette.

00:03:04.534 --> 00:03:06.167
C'mon, get out of here."

00:03:06.167 --> 00:03:10.000
But you can tell it's, like,
not specific enough.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.534
No disrespect to British actors.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:13.300
They all do great.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.434
[ Laughter ]
Phew.

00:03:15.434 --> 00:03:18.968
-I want to ask one more thing,

00:03:18.968 --> 00:03:21.033
because obviously, this
is a trio that we know

00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:23.634
from "The Californians,"
you, Bill, Wiig.

00:03:23.634 --> 00:03:26.334
[ Applause ]

00:03:26.334 --> 00:03:31.133
There's an unseen sketch

00:03:31.133 --> 00:03:33.634
that the same three of you did
called "The Australians."

00:03:33.634 --> 00:03:36.100
-Yes!
-That didn't ever go to dress?

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.767
-It did go to dress, twice.
-It went to dress twice.

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:41.567
A different concept, but explain
"The Australians" real quick.

00:03:41.567 --> 00:03:44.801
-So this is a sketch
you've never seen but

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:47.367
all it was was
just me, Bill, and Kristen

00:03:47.367 --> 00:03:50.100
as Australians packing
to go on a trip.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:54.000
So we've got a big suitcase open
and we're just like,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.400
[Australian accent] "Got to
throw a boomerang in there."

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:59.501
And we just list
Australian things.

00:03:59.501 --> 00:04:02.267
"DVD of 'Muriel's Wedding.'
Alright."

00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:04.601
And that's all it is.

00:04:04.601 --> 00:04:06.100
We just list these
Australian things.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.667
And at the end of it,

00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:09.567
the credits go downwards.

00:04:09.567 --> 00:04:11.467
Like, you know,
sort of opposite.

00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:13.234
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.234 --> 00:04:15.567
-So the way the toilets
flush backwards in Australia?

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:17.200
-The credits, yeah.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.667
And yeah, and that was
the whole sketch

00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:20.868
was just us listing things.

00:04:20.868 --> 00:04:23.133
"Rain gear.
Throw some rain gear in there."

00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:24.501
And that was it.

00:04:24.501 --> 00:04:28.968
-My recollection of it is,
it was very patently clear

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:31.067
about halfway through the sketch
that it was not going well.

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:33.434
And then the three of
you would just start

00:04:33.434 --> 00:04:35.367
enjoying it even more.

00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:36.901
-Even more.
-Because I would say,

00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:39.300
as a trio goes,
in my history of the show,

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.234
the three of you had a sort of
affection for each other

00:04:43.234 --> 00:04:44.968
and so you really were having
just as much fun

00:04:44.968 --> 00:04:46.300
when things were going badly.

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.634
-Yeah, and it was a nice,
simple sketch, but yeah.

00:04:48.634 --> 00:04:51.367
You can really feel -- this is
something you pointed out.

00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:52.834
Sometimes you
blame the audience.

00:04:52.834 --> 00:04:54.467
You're like, "They're just
really quiet tonight."

00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:56.300
But then --

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:57.534
[ Laughter ]

00:04:57.534 --> 00:04:58.767
But then later in the night,

00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:00.400
you'll hear them laugh
at someone else's sketch.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.701
And you're like,
"Oh, they're here."

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:03.767
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-Oh, they were --

00:05:03.767 --> 00:05:06.167
They were just making
a value judgment on me.

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:07.868
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.868 --> 00:05:09.133
-That was specific.

00:05:09.133 --> 00:05:10.601
-Nothing's worse than when
you walk off after a dud

00:05:10.601 --> 00:05:12.267
and you see, like, people going
out for their sketch

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:14.367
and you're like, "Best of luck.
They're real quiet."

00:05:14.367 --> 00:05:15.667
And then like --

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:18.968
-This was one of those, yeah.

00:05:18.968 --> 00:05:20.167
-Thank you so much
for being here.

00:05:20.167 --> 00:05:21.734
Thank you for sticking around.

00:05:21.734 --> 00:05:22.601
I love you very much.
-Love you, too.

00:05:22.601 --> 00:05:23.968
-You guys, that's Fred Armisen.

00:05:23.968 --> 00:05:26.267
"Wednesday,"
currently streaming on Netflix.

00:05:26.267 --> 00:05:27.300
We'll be right back.

